<Project>

  <!--
    Every module package depends on GstSharp.Net, and the analyzers ship inside
    that package. `dotnet pack` writes a reference into the nuspec with the
    default private assets of a project reference, which comes out as
    <dependency id="GstSharp.Net" ... exclude="Build,Analyzers" />: the package
    asks NuGet not to pass its analyzer assets on to whoever depends on a
    module.

    That is the opposite of what this repository wants. A consumer that
    references only GstSharp.Net.Sdp is exactly the one that needs GST0001 and
    GST0002, because a module surface is where the wrappers it forgets to
    dispose come from. It gets them today only because the restore of the
    current SDK does not act on the Analyzers token - measured, with a scratch
    feed: an `exclude="All"` on that same dependency suppresses the compile
    assets, while an `exclude="Analyzers"` suppresses nothing and the rules
    still run. So the rules reach a module-only consumer by accident, not by
    arrangement, and a NuGet that starts honouring the token would take them
    away without anything here changing.

    Clearing PrivateAssets drops the exclude, so the dependency entry says what
    the project means and the outcome stops depending on which tokens the
    restore of the day acts on. It is written here rather than on each of the
    ten references so that a module added later cannot be the one that silently
    loses the rules; the Update matches nothing in the projects that do not
    reference GstSharp.Net.

    This is Directory.Build.targets rather than Directory.Build.props because
    Update only reaches items that already exist, and the props file is
    imported before the project body declares them. The path is the one the
    module projects write, and it is resolved against each of them rather than
    against this file.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Update="../GstSharp.Net/GstSharp.Net.csproj" PrivateAssets="none" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
